Public and Private Inheritance

📌 Description

This C++ program demonstrates the difference between Public Inheritance and Private Inheritance.

The program uses one Base class and two derived classes:

- "PublicDerived"
- "PrivateDerived"

---

📚 Base Class

The "Base" class contains a public function called "show()".

void show() const {
    std::cout << "Base public function\n";
}

This function displays a simple message.

---

🔓 Public Inheritance

class PublicDerived : public Base {
};

The "PublicDerived" class inherits from "Base" using public inheritance.

Therefore, the public function "show()" remains public and can be accessed using an object of "PublicDerived".

PublicDerived publicObject;
publicObject.show();

Output

Base public function

---

🔒 Private Inheritance

class PrivateDerived : private Base {

The "PrivateDerived" class inherits from "Base" using private inheritance.

Because of private inheritance, the public members of "Base" become private members of "PrivateDerived".

Therefore, "show()" cannot be directly accessed outside the class.

// privateObject.show();  // Error

However, "PrivateDerived" can access "show()" internally through its own public function:

void callBaseShow() const {
    show();
}

So, the following is valid:

PrivateDerived privateObject;
privateObject.callBaseShow();

Output

Base public function

